  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Friday)\nIssued at 1238 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nAn upper ridge is expected to amplify over the northern Plains\nthis weekend, downstream from deep troughing along the West Coast.\nOur area will be in a light east to northeast flow on its south\nside. Also, WV and PWAT satellite imagery indicates a pool of\nricher PWAT air to our east and south that will be spreading west.\nThe CAMs today are more optimistic that we will see at least\nspotty afternoon showers and storms over the weekend, likely at\nleast in some part due to the position of the ridge aloft (not\nover our region) and an increase in PWATs (richer deep layer\nmoisture). Some slight chance PoPs were added both Saturday and\nSunday, mainly over eastern areas (starting in the terrain and\nworking west). Used CONSShort and CONSRaw as guidance for PoPs.\n\nAs far as the heat is concerned, forecast temps in the low 100s\nwith the humidity in place will yield daily heat headlines. A heat\nadvisory was issued for Saturday and additional advisories will be\nneeded Sunday on into next week.\n\nUpper heights rise overhead on Monday and will continue to rise \ntoward the middle of next week as the ridge expands in scope \nacross the South/Southeast. Hot and dry weather will prevail.\nToward the end of the week, troughing over the Great Lakes and\nNortheast may be strong enough to force a back door front close\nenough to our area to bring low rain/storm chances close to the KS\nand MO borders. Drought busting rains are unlikely however.\n\nLacy",
